WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:14.000
Norway is famous for its extreme climate of
long sunny summer days, long dark winter nights,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.129
and extreme cold.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:21.950
They are also famous for their welfare state
(like any other nordic country), but perhaps

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:25.750
most of all they are famous for their smoked
salmon!

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:32.590
And it’s not just us who think that - the
fishing industry is one of the most modern

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:34.630
and important in the world...

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:39.180
However, in the last few years, the most significant
element of the Norwegian identity has been

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.200
its commitment to the environment, especially
its fight against climate change.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:48.360
And there is no better place for us to see
this than in their adoption of the electric

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:49.360
car.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.740
You see, Norway has the most developed market
of electric cars in the world.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:57.110
In Norway, one out of every two of the cars
sold is either electric or hybrid!

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.770
But there´s more...

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:05.570
By 2025 (which is basically tomorrow) Norway
expects to stop selling gas or diesel powered

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:06.940
cars.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:10.229
They only want to sell electric and hybrid
cars.

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:11.860
It’s pretty hard to imagine, right?

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:16.380
After more than a 100 years of combustion
engines, it all seems as if it’s about to

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.460
come to an end, at least for this country…

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:24.760
Here, electric “car” mania has even reached
their ships.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.600
Two companies are currently developing a project
called the “Tesla of the Sea”.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.560
Its goal is to launch the first autonomous
ship powered by electric batteries.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.549
The first “Yara Birkeland”, the formal
name of the project, is expected to be ready

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:39.340
by the end of 2018.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:43.310
And this might leave you wondering, well,
what’s Norway´s secret?

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.520
How on Earth have they made electric cars
quite so popular?

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:51.530
Are they really so concerned about the environment?

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:56.600
Well, yeah, maybe that is a part of it.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:00.840
But the majority reason is rather less idealistic…

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.210
The key for this electric car boom has everything
to do with the tax incentives that the government

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.069
has granted to support this industry.

00:02:09.069 --> 00:02:14.530
Look, in Norway, when you need to buy a car,
you can save a fortune in taxes if you choose

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.920
and electric vehicle.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.590
For instance, you don't have to pay any VAT
(and in Norway that’s a 25% right off the

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:22.590
bat).

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.349
You also don’t pay for the registration
fee or any other additional taxes.

00:02:26.349 --> 00:02:31.860
So what’s the result of all of this financially?

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.730
Well, according to a recent study, a Tesla
Model S, for instance, would cost around $140,000

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:45.120
if all the taxes gas powered cars have to
pay for were to be paid on it, but thanks

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.480
to this tax relief, the price is actually
around $70,000.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.489
50% in savings!

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:53.489
Pretty good, right?

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:58.799
Oh, and then there are the other advantages...

00:02:58.799 --> 00:03:03.620
these cars do not have to pay for tolls or
even parking meters.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:08.980
Further, in Norway, most of the electricity
is hydroelectric, which means it is both cheap

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:09.999
and clean.

00:03:09.999 --> 00:03:15.829
As a result for these and other policies,
the government expects to reduce carbon emissions

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:18.099
by 40% in the next few years.

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:23.750
So, now you know this, and also if you consider
that this is one of the countries with the

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:28.739
highest living standards in the world...you
might be thinking...Well, wow, what a great

00:03:28.739 --> 00:03:29.739
country!

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:34.969
Even the Norwegian constitution itself is
concerned with the environment.

00:03:34.969 --> 00:03:39.339
Consider article 110:
“Every person has a right to an environment

00:03:39.339 --> 00:03:43.409
that is conducive to health and to a natural
environment whose productivity and diversity

00:03:43.409 --> 00:03:44.680
are maintained.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:49.069
Natural resources should be managed on the
basis of comprehensive long-term considerations

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:55.249
whereby this right will be safeguarded for
future generations as well” Article 110b

00:03:55.249 --> 00:03:57.180
of Norway´s Constitution.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:03.389
However, as always, not everything can be
sunshine, lollipops and rainbows...

00:04:03.389 --> 00:04:07.489
Norway is a wealthy country, with a huge welfare
state.

00:04:07.489 --> 00:04:09.760
BUT they do have a small issue…

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.379
Or, at least, a problem that makes Greenpeace
really angry:

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:18.610
And that issue is about the oil production
of the country.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:22.360
But, really, how oil-dependent is Norway?

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.050
Well, let’s take a look...

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:28.770
OIL ADDICTS?

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:33.949
If we could take a time machine back to the
beginning of the 20th Century, we would find

00:04:33.949 --> 00:04:36.599
Norway with a very basic economy.

00:04:36.599 --> 00:04:39.660
All they did was fishing, fishing and more
fishing.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.639
If we travelled forward in time and, even
though things improved significantly, Norway

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:48.460
during the 1960s was in no way included in
the list of the wealthiest countries...

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.460
not at all.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.500
In fact, its per capita income was around
half the amount of Switzerland's income.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.199
“In 1960, the standard of living in Norway
was about 30 or 40 percent lower than in Sweden

00:04:57.199 --> 00:04:58.199
or in Denmark.

00:04:58.199 --> 00:05:02.060
Now the standard of living in Norway is substantially
higher than in those two countries."

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:05.800
Bruno Gerard, an economist at the Norwegian
Business School in Oslo.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.129
So now we must ask, well, what happened?

00:05:08.129 --> 00:05:12.379
How did Norway become one of the richest countries
on Earth in so little time?

00:05:12.379 --> 00:05:19.409
Well, it all began to change in 1969, the
year when the first oil reserves were found.

00:05:19.409 --> 00:05:22.280
And, since then, it’s been a bit of a golden
ear.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:25.539
The country became one of the world's largest
oil and natural gas suppliers.

00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:30.500
And, as you can imagine, this oil revolution
meant tons of money coming in - money that

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:36.120
contributed to building the Norway we know
today.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:40.979
Today, oil and natural gas are keys of this
country's economy.

00:05:40.979 --> 00:05:45.969
In 2016, for instance, almost 40% of exports
and more than a fifth of investment had its

00:05:45.969 --> 00:05:47.970
origin in the oil and gas sector.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:51.000
And, of course, the government gets its share…

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.409
Look, depending on the year and the prices
of gas and oil, the income the government

00:05:55.409 --> 00:06:01.800
obtains from this industry varies from 1 out
of 4 to 1 out of 6 Euros of the government´s

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:02.800
tax collection.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:03.919
That is A LOT of money.

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:05.800
And more public income usually means…

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.639
more public spending.

00:06:07.639 --> 00:06:12.940
“World's Most Generous Welfare State Leaves
20% on the Dole”Yep, that’s right.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:17.499
To a certain extent, we could say the Norwegian
model of a huge welfare state with public

00:06:17.499 --> 00:06:21.460
control of many of the large companies, has
a lot to do with oil.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:26.009
However, the fall in the prices of this raw
materials has led the government to take a

00:06:26.009 --> 00:06:27.729
very controversial measure:

00:06:27.729 --> 00:06:33.499
For the first time in 20 years, the government
has granted new exploration licences.

00:06:33.499 --> 00:06:38.409
In particular, 10 new licences of oil exploration
in the Barents Sea, in the heart of the Arctic

00:06:38.409 --> 00:06:41.159
Circle.

00:06:41.159 --> 00:06:51.319
And the truth is, this movement has made Greenpeace
and other environmental organizations really

00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:52.319
angry.

00:06:52.319 --> 00:06:56.680
Greenpeace has, by the way, taken the government
to court accusing them of violating the Paris

00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:01.199
Agreement and also of violating the Norwegian
Constitution itself.

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:05.210
As we already told you, the environment is
well protected in Norwegian law.

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:10.610
But the thing is, Norway´s green commitment
has certain limitations...

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.779
“Norway has set out to be a global leader
in climate action, yet continued expansion

00:07:14.779 --> 00:07:19.319
of oil and gas production could eclipse the
benefits of Norway’s domestic emission reduction

00:07:19.319 --> 00:07:20.319
efforts”.

00:07:20.319 --> 00:07:25.020
Peter Erickson, a senior scientist with the
Stockholm Environment Institute.And the reality

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:31.710
is that the world’s largest welfare state
does not come cheap…We, as a country, we

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.160
are petroholics [...] we depend so much on
the income from the oil.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.870
Just like alcoholics, we do want to stop,
but we don’t know how.”

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:40.729
Thomas Nilsen, Editor of the Independent Barents
Observer.

00:07:40.729 --> 00:07:48.120
But, hey, it is not as if Norway was like
those other countries that only rely on oil.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:49.120
Absolutely not.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.330
The country is dependent on oil to a certain
extent, but it is not even close to how dependent

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:56.129
Venezuela or Saudi Arabia are.

00:07:56.129 --> 00:07:58.469
In Norway, things are very different.

00:07:58.469 --> 00:08:03.279
Although the government carries a lot of economic
weight, Norway has tried to be open to business,

00:08:03.279 --> 00:08:08.150
and they have tried to use all this oil money
to fund productive activities and to provide

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:09.520
a quality education.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.630
They’ve also been very committed to saving.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:19.509
THE FUND OF FUNDS

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:24.020
For a country that has made so much money
with the oil industry, Norway is very different

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:25.020
to what we are used to.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:29.529
Oslo, the capital city, is not taken over
by sky-scrapers, or massive malls, or extravagant

00:08:29.529 --> 00:08:30.529
complexes.

00:08:30.529 --> 00:08:33.520
In other words, they may have spent a lot
of money, but the key to Norway is, probably,

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.110
that they haven't wasted it (as it has happened
in other countries.)

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:40.050
The Norwegian government made the sensible
decision to save a lot of their oil money.

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:44.920
They set up a sovereign wealth fund to invest
the money, and buy assets all around the globe.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:48.940
And this is the biggest sovereign wealth fund
on Earth.

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:53.320
Not even Saudi Arabia and Kuwait have saved
so much.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:57.520
Consider this:
“Norway's Sovereign Wealth Fund Hits $1

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.300
Trillion” ForbesA.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:00.300
Trillion.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:01.300
Dollars.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:06.070
This is a fortune of around $190,000 per citizen.

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:09.779
Instead of wasting the oil money, they decided
to invest in companies all around the world.

00:09:09.779 --> 00:09:14.190
This fund has invested money in more than
9,000 companies in 78 countries.

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:18.390
Some of these companies are really famous:
Apple, Nestlé, Shell or Alphabet (better

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.390
known as Google).

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:24.260
Yes, indeed, we could say that the Norwegians
own part of YouTube!

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:25.260
And you know what?

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:31.459
One of their targets is to eventually replace
their oil income with income from these investments.

00:09:31.459 --> 00:09:35.110
These investments they all produce profit
(as you can probably imagine)...

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:37.730
Well, since the creation of the Norwegian
Sovereign Wealth Fund, the average annual

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:38.889
return has been around 5.7%.

00:09:38.889 --> 00:09:43.240
So, if this level of return was to be kept
the same, Norway would get an average of $50

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:44.279
billion in profit.

00:09:44.279 --> 00:09:48.279
That’s equivalent to 25% of their public
spending.

00:09:48.279 --> 00:09:49.279
Sound good, right?

00:09:49.279 --> 00:09:53.950
On top of that we must add the income the
government gets from oil and local companies…

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:57.779
Companies that, despite being state-owned,
work with market criteria.

00:09:57.779 --> 00:10:02.880
So, well, you might be thinking that Norway
has nothing to worry about, right?

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:04.339
Well, wait just a second.

00:10:04.339 --> 00:10:09.490
The welfare level seems to have a solid base,
but the government is determined to achieve

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:14.460
economic diversification, in order to have
a more competitive economy, so they ultimately

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:21.610
get rid off their dependency on oil.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:25.350
THE REFORMS

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:30.660
Since 1927, the centre-left Labour Party has
been the most popular in general elections.

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.690
But in Norway, absolute majorities are not
common.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:38.250
In 2013, a coalition of centre-right wing
parties like the Progress Party (famous for

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:42.220
its radical position on immigration) managed
to put Erna Solberg in office.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:45.740
She is known as the Norwegian version of Angela
Merkel.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:51.500
Recently, in the elections of 11th September
2017, this coalition was re-elected.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:55.600
The intention of the government is to get
some distance from what has been known as

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.190
the Norwegian mode.

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:01.630
The government wants to improve the competitiveness
of the country by reducing taxes, especially

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:03.899
for companies (both public and private).

00:11:03.899 --> 00:11:10.970
But, they also intend to keep developing the
oil industry.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:19.220
Anyway, if they are sure about something in
Norway, it is that innovation and development

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:24.779
are the best ways to obtain high standards
of living and improve the environment.

00:11:24.779 --> 00:11:25.920
So, now it is your turn.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.889
Do you think Norway is an example to be followed
in the management of natural resources?

00:11:30.889 --> 00:11:34.759
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

00:11:34.759 --> 00:11:38.480
Don’t forget to subscribe to our channel
for brand new videos every Monday and Thursday.

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:44.240
Also, don’t forget to check out our friends
at the Reconsider Media Podcast - they provided

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:52.600
the vocals in this episode that were not mine!

00:11:52.600 --> 00:12:31.509
And 
as always, thanks for watching!

